# 3. `add()`

## LEVEL 1

`add()` is used to add **one element** to a collection.

```java
c.add(10);
```

---

## LEVEL 2

Suppose:

```text
[]
```

After:

```java
c.add(10);
```

we get:

```text
[10]
```

Then:

```java
c.add(20);
```

gives:

```text
[10, 20]
```

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c = new ArrayList();

        c.add(10);
        c.add(20);
        c.add(30);

        System.out.println(c);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
[10, 20, 30]
```

### Remember

```text
add()
 ↓
ONE element
```

Return type:

```text
boolean
```

---

# 4. `addAll()`

## LEVEL 1

`addAll()` adds the elements of one collection into another collection.

```java
c1.addAll(c2);
```

---

## LEVEL 2

Suppose:

```text
c1 = [10, 20]
c2 = [30, 40]
```

Then:

```java
c1.addAll(c2);
```

Result:

```text
c1 = [10, 20, 30, 40]
```

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c1 = new ArrayList();
        Collection c2 = new ArrayList();

        c1.add(10);
        c1.add(20);

        c2.add(30);
        c2.add(40);

        c1.addAll(c2);

        System.out.println(c1);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
[10, 20, 30, 40]
```

### Remember

```text
add()     → one element

addAll()  → elements from another collection
```

Return type:

```text
boolean
```

---

# 5. `remove()`

## LEVEL 1

`remove()` removes an element from the collection.

```java
c.remove(20);
```

---

## LEVEL 2

Before:

```text
[10, 20, 30]
```

After:

```java
c.remove(20);
```

Result:

```text
[10, 30]
```

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c = new ArrayList();

        c.add(10);
        c.add(20);
        c.add(30);

        System.out.println(c);

        c.remove(20);

        System.out.println(c);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
[10, 20, 30]
[10, 30]
```

### Important

At the `Collection` interface level:

```java
c.remove(20);
```

means remove the matching element.

Later, when we study `List`, we will encounter the famous:

```java
list.remove(1);
```

index-vs-value confusion.

---

# 6. `removeAll()`

## LEVEL 1

`removeAll()` removes elements from one collection that are also present in another collection.

```java
c1.removeAll(c2);
```

---

## LEVEL 2

Suppose:

```text
c1 = [10, 20, 30, 40]

c2 = [20, 40]
```

Execute:

```java
c1.removeAll(c2);
```

Result:

```text
c1 = [10, 30]
```

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c1 = new ArrayList();
        Collection c2 = new ArrayList();

        c1.add(10);
        c1.add(20);
        c1.add(30);
        c1.add(40);

        c2.add(20);
        c2.add(40);

        c1.removeAll(c2);

        System.out.println(c1);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
[10, 30]
```

### Direction is important

```java
c1.removeAll(c2);
```

means:

```text
Remove from c1
the elements that are present in c2.
```

It does **not** clear `c2`.

