# 7. `contains()`

## LEVEL 1

`contains()` checks whether a particular element exists.

```java
c.contains(20);
```

---

## LEVEL 2

If:

```text
[10, 20, 30]
```

then:

```java
c.contains(20);
```

returns:

```text
true
```

But:

```java
c.contains(50);
```

returns:

```text
false
```

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c = new ArrayList();

        c.add(10);
        c.add(20);
        c.add(30);

        System.out.println(c.contains(20));
        System.out.println(c.contains(50));
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
true
false
```

### Remember

```text
contains()
    ↓
"Is this element present?"
```

Return type:

```text
boolean
```

---

# 8. `containsAll()`

## LEVEL 1

`containsAll()` checks whether **all elements** of another collection are present.

```java
c1.containsAll(c2);
```

---

## LEVEL 2

Suppose:

```text
c1 = [10, 20, 30, 40]

c2 = [20, 40]
```

Then:

```java
c1.containsAll(c2);
```

returns:

```text
true
```

Because both `20` and `40` are present.

If:

```text
c2 = [20, 50]
```

then:

```java
c1.containsAll(c2);
```

returns:

```text
false
```

because `50` is missing.

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c1 = new ArrayList();
        Collection c2 = new ArrayList();

        c1.add(10);
        c1.add(20);
        c1.add(30);
        c1.add(40);

        c2.add(20);
        c2.add(40);

        System.out.println(c1.containsAll(c2));
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
true
```

### Remember

```text
contains()       → ONE element

containsAll()    → ALL elements
```

