# 12. `iterator()`

## LEVEL 1

`iterator()` is used to obtain an `Iterator` object.

```java
Iterator itr = c.iterator();
```

It is used to traverse the collection.

---

## LEVEL 2

Suppose:

```text
[10, 20, 30]
```

The Iterator moves through the collection:

```text
10 → 20 → 30
```

Two important Iterator methods are:

```java
itr.hasNext()
itr.next()
```

`hasNext()` asks:

```text
"Is another element available?"
```

`next()` gets the next element.

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c = new ArrayList();

        c.add(10);
        c.add(20);
        c.add(30);

        Iterator itr = c.iterator();

        while(itr.hasNext())
        {
            System.out.println(itr.next());
        }
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
10
20
30
```

### Mental picture

```text
Collection
[10] [20] [30]
  ↑
 Iterator

hasNext()
   ↓
next()
   ↓
10

hasNext()
   ↓
next()
   ↓
20

hasNext()
   ↓
next()
   ↓
30

hasNext()
   ↓
false
```

### Important

```java
c.iterator()
```

does **not** return an element.

It returns:

```text
Iterator object
```

---

# 13. `toArray()`

## LEVEL 1

`toArray()` converts the elements of the collection into an array.

---

## LEVEL 2

Suppose:

```text
Collection
[10, 20, 30]
```

Then:

```java
Object arr[] = c.toArray();
```

produces:

```text
Object[]
[10, 20, 30]
```

---

## LEVEL 3 — Program

```java
import java.util.*;

class Demo
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Collection c = new ArrayList();

        c.add(10);
        c.add(20);
        c.add(30);

        Object arr[] = c.toArray();

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++)
        {
            System.out.println(arr[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
10
20
30
```

### Remember

```text
Collection
    ↓
toArray()
    ↓
Object[]
```

